Section I: Review Questions
1.(a) What is the difference between a compiler and an interpreter?
  A compiler translates an entire program into an executable, standalone file. This allows it to display all errors found within the file at once, due to it having read through the entire program. 
  An interpreter on the other hand, translates the file while it executes said file, while not generating a separate file. If the interpreter encounters any issues, it stops at the first one it encounters.
(b) What is the output of a C program’s main() function by default?
  By default, the main() funciton returns an integer '0' by default.
2. What are header files in C and what is the purpose of the #include directive?
  A header file is a file that contains function declarations, constraints, and type definitions. It's like a library, that is user defined. The actual implemented function is stored in an accompanying .c file.
  The #include directive is used to tell a compiler to include the contents of another file, like those found in a library or header file, when the program is compiled. 
3. Explain how to declare and define a function in C. What is the purpose of the return statement in a function? Can a function have more than one return statement?
  A function must be declared in the following format: "<function_return_type> <function_name>(<arguement_type> <Argument_name>)". It may contain more than one arguments, with each being separated by a comma. A funciton is defined by the code written within the program's brackets, with it returning a value matching what had been inittally declared.
  The return statement ends the execution of the function, while potentially sending a value bakc to the line of code that initially called the function itself.
  A function can have more that one return statement, though it must be structured in a manner that allows to execute only one at a time. Like when a function implements an if-else statment, with each branch containing its own return statement.
4. What is type casting? Provide an example C function that demonstrates explicit type casting from double to int. The function should accept two arguments that are both double and return their sum as an integer.
Type casting is when you change a value from one data type into another.
Example:
int addNewtype (double num, double num1){
  return (int)(num + num1);
}
5. Explain the difference between local and global variables. Provide an example of each.
Local variables are variables in a program that are declared within a specific function, and are only accessed within that function as a result. Global variables are variables declared outside of all functions, generally at the top level of the program.
This renders it accessable to all
Example of a local variable:
void number(void){
  int num = 2;
  printf("My number is: %d", num);
}
Example of a global variable:
#include <stdio.h>
int num = 2;
void printNum(void){
    printf("%d\n", num);
}
int main(void){
    printNum();
    num = 4;
    printNum();
    return 0;
}
6. How are strings declared and initialized in C? What is the role of the null terminator ‘\0’?
strings are declared and initialized like this:
char name[] = "Yes";
char name[4] = "Yes";
char name[] = {'Y', 'e', 's', '\0'};
This is due to the fact that C doesn't have a built in string data type, resulting in strings getting stored as an array of characters.
A null terminator is used to mark the end of a string.
7. What is a pointer in C? How do you pass a pointer to a function? What advantages are there to passing a pointer instead of a value?
A pointer is a variable that contians the memory address of another variable. 
This is how one would pass a pointer to a function:
void change(int *ptr){
  *ptr = 2;
}
int main(void){
  int x = 4;
  change(&x);
  printf("%d\n", x);
  return 0;
}
The main advantage of pointers is that it allows for one to directly change the value of an original variable, regardless of its location.
Calling a variable within a function results in the function receiving a copy of the variable, not the actual variable itself.
8. What does the * operator and the & operator do in the context of pointers?
The * operator acceses the value stored at the mrmory address contained in a pointer. The & operator gets the memory address of a variable.
9. What is the difference between while and do…while loops?
10. What does the break statement do? How is it different from the continue statement?
The break statement immediately exits a loop. It results whatever is found right outside of the loop getting executed.
The continue statement ends the current iteration of the loop, but doesn't exit it. Instead, it immediately start another iteration.
11. Explain the use of bitwise operators (i.e. &, |, ^, ~, <<, >>) in C. Which bitwise operators can be used to set, clear, toggle, or check a specific bit in an integer variable?
Bitwise operators allow one to modify specific bits of a variable in the following:
& is a logic "and", resulting in 1 if both bits are 1. Used to clear bits alongside ~. Also used to check bits.
| is a logic "or", resulting in 1 if either bit is 1. Used to set bits.
^ is a logic "xor", resulting in 1 if the bits are different. Used to toggle bits.
~ is a logic "not", resulting in an inversion of bits. Used to clear bits alongside &.
<< is a left shift, in which the bits are shifted to the left.
>> is a right shift, in which the bits are shifted to the right.
12. What is the purpose of the PxSEL0 and PxSEL1 GPIO registers? Write two statements that select the GPIO function for the pins P1.0 and P1.7.
THese registers are used to configure the function of the selected pints.
PxSEL0[0] = 0;
PxSEL1[0] = 0;
PxSEL0[7] = 0;
PxSEL1[7] = 0;
